# Line-ending policy: LF in the repository, for every text file.
#
# Without this, whether a file lands as CRLF or LF depends on the
# contributor's platform and their core.autocrlf setting. The result was 92
# files stored with CRLF while the other 8,483 used LF -- and any tool that
# normalises endings (a formatter, an editor's save hook, a scripted edit)
# rewrites every line of one of those files. That turns a 13-line change into
# a 5,000-line diff, which buries the real change in review and makes
# git blame useless for that file.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` tells git to normalise on commit and check out LF
# everywhere, including on Windows. Modern Windows tooling -- VS Code, PyCharm,
# Notepad, PowerShell -- all handle LF, so there is no reason to keep CRLF in
# the tree.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary files: never touch these. Listing them explicitly avoids relying on
# git's heuristic, which can misread a file as text and corrupt it.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.svg   text eol=lf
*.pdf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.mo    binary
*.zip   binary
*.gz    binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.xls   binary
*.db    binary
*.sqlite3 binary

# Windows-only scripts genuinely need CRLF to run.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
